<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    dmux managed WezTerm mux server (plan §15.1, ADR 002).
    launchd is the ONLY legitimate starter: WezTerm has no unix-socket mutual
    exclusion, so a manual wezterm-mux-server on the same socket silently
    steals it. Install: launchctl bootstrap gui/$UID <this file>.
    Restart: launchctl kickstart -k gui/$UID/com.fredrir.wezterm-mux.
    Canary / cutover flag (plan §21 steps 7 and 9, ADR 012 WS-F.1): write
    DMUX_WEZ_FIRST=1 to the untracked ~/.config/dmux/service.env. The wrapper
    reads that file itself, and the com.fredrir.dmux-env LaunchAgent copies it
    into the launchd session at login for the GUI -- `launchctl setenv` alone
    does not survive a reboot, which is how Macie lost its canary on
    2026-08-19. With no value anywhere, the wrapper defaults to the inert
    flag-off path. Emergency opt-out (ADR 010 §2/§5): DMUX_LEGACY_POLICY=1 in
    the same file (or launchctl setenv for one session) forces dmux back to
    legacy tmux creation even while DMUX_WEZ_FIRST=1 is still set, so a
    canary host escapes without first unsetting what it exported. It reverses creation policy only -- this job
    keeps running and its panes keep living, because §21's rollback says to
    leave a live mux server alone. Shells started before the setenv keep the
    old environment; restart them.
  -->
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.fredrir.wezterm-mux</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/Users/fredrir/dotfiles/shared/wezterm/mux/dmux-mux-start.sh</string>
  </array>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>
  <key>ProcessType</key>
  <string>Background</string>
  <!-- Do not ask launchd to open predictable /tmp log paths: that would
       follow a preplanted symlink before the service's native retained
       runtime capability exists. Operational state is published through the
       verified descriptor/recovery status instead. -->
</dict>
</plist>
